          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>Stars have always been there, but these days, seeing them can take some work. Artificial light from homes, highways, businesses, and billboards now washes out much of the night sky, leaving many Americans with a better view of the nearest Walmart sign than the Milky Way.</p><p><br></p><p>Thankfully, Appalachia still has places where darkness settles in thick. From a mountaintop astronomy field in Pennsylvania to a high-elevation bald in North Carolina, these destinations offer front-row seats to the oldest show on Earth.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

          <div id="znid-477787923115"
