              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>At more than 10,000 acres, Watoga is West Virginia’s largest state park. It is also among its darkest. Watoga, Calvin Price State Forest, and Droop Mountain Battlefield State Park collectively hold an International Dark Sky Park designation, creating one mighty dark corner of the Mountain State.</p><p><span style="color: inherit; font-family: inherit; font-size: inherit; letter-spacing: 0px; text-align: inherit;"><br></span></p><p><span style="color: inherit; font-family: inherit; font-size: inherit; letter-spacing: 0px; text-align: inherit;">Watoga Dam is a favorite viewing spot, offering a wide-open look at the sky with a little starlight reflected in the lake for extra drama. On a clear, moonless night, the Milky Way cuts a pale path overhead, while planets, constellations, and meteor showers can often be spotted without hauling along a telescope the size of a water heater.</span></p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

